+# Proposal
+
+## Why
+
+Our services need to change stored JSON documents (configuration, API payloads, records) with a standard, interoperable patch format rather than ad-hoc code. The library already resolves JSON Pointers (RFC 6901), which is the addressing layer JSON Patch builds on.
+
+## What Changes
+
+- Add `applyPatch(document, patch)`, exported from `src/index.ts`, implementing JSON Patch with its six operations (`add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy`, `test`).
+- It returns the patched document as a new value, never mutates `document` or `patch`, and throws a `JsonPatchError` when the patch is invalid or any operation fails (no partial result is returned).
+- Not breaking: purely additive; `resolvePointer` and `JsonPointerError` are unchanged.
+
+**Governing standard:** JSON Patch, RFC 6902 (§3 documents, §4 operations, §4.6 test equality, §5 error handling), using JSON Pointer, RFC 6901, for `path` and `from`.
+
+**Out of scope:** JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7396), generating a patch by diffing two documents, the `application/json-patch+json` media type / HTTP handling, URI fragment pointers, and any operations beyond the six in RFC 6902.
+
+## Capabilities
+
+### New Capabilities
+- `json-patch`: applying an RFC 6902 JSON Patch to a JSON document without mutating inputs, with strict validation and failure semantics.
+
+### Modified Capabilities
+
+## Impact
+
+- Code: `src/index.ts` (new export and error class); new tests in `test/patch.test.ts`; README gains a JSON Patch section.
+- No new dependencies; `package.json` unchanged.
